00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.420
So I've been at Capital One
for about the past seven years.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.420
Over that time, I've held a
number of different roles.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.130
But fortunately, both
the data science toolbox

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.490
and, specifically, R has really
been a through line for me

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.530
throughout whatever
I've been doing.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:36.000
Spent some time on the
more strategic analytics

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.110
and modeling spaces, solving
very specific business problems

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.688
related to our card business.

00:02:42.688 --> 00:02:44.730
I'd say, overall, I think
my career has pulled me

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.970
kind of progressively further
and further up the data stack,

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.810
as no matter what problem
downstream I'm really motivated

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:55.950
by solving, I always realize
there's some roadblock just one

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:59.230
level higher, and I get
really excited about, well,

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:00.970
how do I just solve
that one pain point?

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:02.340
And that one, I'll unlock.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.110
So, after starting out more in
that first-line data science

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.960
space, moved a little
bit further upstream

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.450
when I got really
passionate about InnerSource

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:16.440
tooling and bringing the best
of what I saw from the outside

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:21.120
our community into my company
and building internal packages

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.370
to help us solve common
business problems,

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.650
like customer lifetime
value modeling,

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:29.310
setting and forecasting
KPIs, and even connecting

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.070
to different enterprise systems.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.280
And really enjoyed kind
of like building out

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.480
both those packages,
but also really

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.320
figuring out that you needed to
also build out an inner source

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:47.130
community around them, again
copying so much from what I've

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:53.550
learned from #rstats
Twitter, and then

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:58.380
kind of continuing that
trajectory upstream.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:03:59.850
Moved more and
more progressively

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:03.090
into also adding in elements of
analytics engineering and data

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:07.410
engineering to
tackle standardizing,

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:11.790
harmonizing, breaking silos
in that raw data input that

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.610
would then flow through the
tools and the ultimate data

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:15.910
products.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:24.780
So that's kind of my trajectory
and where I've landed today.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:25.530
Awesome.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:29.880
And what's something you like
to do outside of work, too?

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:34.070
Big, big runner
and big reader.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:34.570
Nice.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:39.440
We'll have to ask you about some
favorite books later on, too.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.020
But I love that you
just started off

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.670
with talking about
InnerSource there,

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:46.330
and that's something that
actually came up on a Hangout--

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:50.080
maybe that was last year, was
Zac Garland at Mastercard.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.500
And I'm curious, for
all of us here, too,

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:56.110
what does InnerSource
really mean?

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.750
And can you explain a bit
about what that community

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.075
looks like at Capital One?

00:05:01.075 --> 00:05:02.870
Yeah, definitely.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:07.360
So in some ways, it's
almost a funny thing

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.390
to explain because I think
to the Open Source Community,

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:12.970
it sounds so natural,
it's almost like,

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.520
why does this concept
require its own name?

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.630
But I think sometimes
in large corporations

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:22.570
or just in different
environments,

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:25.930
you don't just have
that nice synergy where

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.120
someone sitting
halfway in the world

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.150
as you has happened to
already solved your problem

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:34.180
and put the answer up on GitHub.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.640
And so there can be
a lot of, I think,

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.180
redundant work, both in
one person's project--

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.580
like moving from
project to project--

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.210
or different teams solving
very similar problems--

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.760
maybe sometimes
reinventing the wheel

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.880
on even the framework for
solving that problems,

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:55.030
other times using the
exact same framework

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:57.310
but rewriting the
same boilerplate code,

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:01.450
like for hyperparameter
tuning, or something like that.

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:03.910
So InnerSource is
just really the idea

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:10.100
of both building tools
and an internal code base

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:13.530
that multiple teams can
contribute to over time.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:18.260
And, as I mentioned,
that only also

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:22.700
works if you try to build up
that same sort of community,

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:28.340
and enthusiasm, and inclusivity,
and knowledge sharing around it

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:31.420
to support it.

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.590
And so that's with a lot
of the internal packages

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:36.390
that you create too?

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:37.930
Yeah, definitely.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.390
So, yeah, a lot of--

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:46.070
I think you can think about the
definition pretty expansively,

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:47.315
being anything from--

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:52.910
I mean, there's so
many opportunities

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:55.430
I think to standardize
across the data stack.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:58.430
But I think the place I've
definitely focused on it most

00:06:58.430 --> 00:06:59.780
is in the R package space.

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:04.050
I love that.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:07.410
So, hopefully, one package
for one thing, and then

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:11.400
everybody across Capital
One is using it for that?

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:12.240
Yeah.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.550
That's always been the goal.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.320
And I think the other
really cool thing

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:24.030
about that is really digging
into the levels of abstraction

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:28.580
and that not--

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:31.520
you, obviously, never want
to constrict different teams

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:33.620
from like solving
problems in different ways

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:35.810
because different
parts of the business

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:38.810
inherently should be
customizing and tailoring.

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:41.820
But it becomes a really
fun problem thinking

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.580
what should be standardized.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:46.100
Like, not everyone wants
to write the glue code

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:49.190
to connect to database and
get through your proxy.

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:52.010
But where are the right
degrees of freedom

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:57.020
of what optimization method
might be truly better for one

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:59.060
type of problem than another?

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:02.770
And then it also,
I think, helps you

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:04.960
aggregate those
business-use cases

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.250
and create that kind of
body of knowledge of these

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:13.120
are how different teams have
solved this different problems,

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.790
why they needed to layer
in different tools.

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:18.640
And it becomes kind
of a knowledge store,

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:20.890
I think, for some of the
complexity of any given

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:22.760
problem.

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:26.030
I'm always really
interested in how

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:29.750
you can standardize
across standardized things

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:32.600
but also share new
packages or share

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:38.120
ways of doing things across
such a huge company, too.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.670
Yeah, no, I mean,
I think definitely--

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.680
as best you can, I
think a lot of companies

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.940
do use a version control
system, like GitLab or GitHub.

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:52.550
So obviously, first
step minimum bar

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:54.230
is just getting it out there.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:56.540
But I think there's a
great little O'Reiley

00:08:56.540 --> 00:09:00.950
book on InnerSource that
one of the chapter headlines

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:03.770
is literally, like, just because
you use GitHub doesn't mean

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:05.870
you're doing InnerSource,
which I think

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:09.710
is really critical that
I think in open source,

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:13.550
we are sometimes spoiled by the
extent to which it is if you

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:15.470
build it, they will come.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:17.430
They will not always
come internally.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:21.770
I think corporate
incentives are different.

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:24.080
Sometimes, the
culture people just

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.820
have no expectation
that if they look,

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:27.530
they might find something.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:32.690
So I think, definitely, there's
a little bit more shoe leather

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:35.990
beat the pavement in terms
of talking to other teams,

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:39.020
trying to go present
it in internal forums.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:42.740
But also, again, building up
that community with things

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:43.730
like--

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:47.900
I leaned very heavily on Slack
channels to kind of bootstrap

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:51.620
an internal version
of Twitter to try

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:56.470
to get those just serendipitous,
async conversations going.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.180
Yeah, definitely.

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:00.580
Libby, I see you had asked
something in the chat

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:01.180
a bit earlier.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:03.540
Do you want to jump in?

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:04.320
Sure, yeah.

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:07.860
I was wondering if you could
describe in general terms,

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:10.770
without giving a bunch of
details that you can't give,

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:13.170
maybe some of the
projects or the problems

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:16.830
that you are working on solving
with your team right now?

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:18.460
Yeah, definitely.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:24.370
So I think pretty
wide range of things,

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:27.160
and I'm trying to figure
out how to answer that,

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:30.900
to be perfectly honest, at the
right level of abstraction.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:37.250
But I think something that's
really been top of mind

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:43.120
for me lately is just how you
can really shape that raw data

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:48.100
layer and make it the most
usable for the [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:50.180
last mile analysis.

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:54.460
So one broad area of
interest that I'll say

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:56.770
has been really on
my mind is thinking

00:10:56.770 --> 00:11:01.960
about how to structure raw
data into really meaningful--

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:04.870
you could almost call it like
a metric SMART or feature MART,

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:07.030
although it's not
quite the same thing.

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:12.550
But just how you can embed
more context about a business

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:15.610
problem in individual
data elements

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:21.040
to have more of those off the
shelf for use kind of across--

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:26.560
metrics tracking across modeling
and feature engineering.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:29.380
Some of the business areas
that I'm most focused

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:35.740
on these days in our code
space is our [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:39.280
to practice
strategies to help be

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:47.530
sure we're equipping our
customers to have the best

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:50.200
both financial
health but ability

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:51.970
to pursue their interests.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:57.030
Thank you.

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:59.570
I'm starting to see a lot of
questions over in the chat,

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:00.070
too.

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:02.720
And, Mike, I swear I did
not see your question there

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:04.670
when I asked that,
so maybe I'm starting

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:07.730
to think a data science
leader a little bit more here.

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:12.710
But, Mike, did you have any
follow-up questions or anything

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:15.830
else you want to get
out of communicating

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:17.990
InnerSource packages
and best practices?

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:18.770
Yeah, thanks.

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:21.590
I mean, first of all,
it's great having Emily

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:23.150
on this Data Science thing.

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:25.460
I've been following your
blog posts for ages,

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:27.060
and they're really
inspirational.

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:29.210
So please keep doing those.

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:31.100
The question was
really about what

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:35.690
I see a lot is that when
you send out an email,

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:39.380
or send out a message on
Teams, or have a community

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:43.490
meeting, maybe you only get
20% of the whole population

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:46.040
of people you're trying to
target at that one thing.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:50.210
And there's value in spamming
people across many platforms,

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:53.390
but I also know that many
of the people that I want

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:55.520
to try and target with
here's a best practice,

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:57.650
or I've written this
document, or here's

00:12:57.650 --> 00:13:00.290
a package you might be
interested in, some of them

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:04.490
have already kind of muted
that channel or an email

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:07.230
rule that just, I'll chuck
about straight into this folder.

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:11.090
So how do you gradually
increment the number of people

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:13.650
who see those messages?

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:14.420
Yeah.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:20.930
I think, definitely, in
an enterprise setting,

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:25.040
I think I have also learned
more also on those personal

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:28.970
relationships to get the word
out than you would necessarily

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:31.190
in the open-source context--

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:36.410
so both in terms of I
think leaning on leadership

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:40.940
to help identify people that may
be working on similar projects

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:44.030
or patterns throughout
the company.

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:47.690
Or, ideally, I think
having a forum where you're

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:52.200
intended to share updates
not on an individual project

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:54.200
because it's like, how
do you get people to come

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:55.700
to that forum initially?

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:59.000
But if you can get people
interested in the idea

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:03.320
of technical knowledge
sharing at a higher level,

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:09.180
aggregating many types of
information in that one space,

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:14.580
again, to make an open-source
analogy that kind of internal R

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:16.530
open Ci analog or internal--

00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:19.410
R weekly-type
newsletter, I think

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:25.120
it can be easier to
sell and socialize

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:30.250
the broader concept than maybe
an individual artifact thereof.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:36.620
Yeah, thanks.

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:37.940
Thank you.

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:39.840
Marlene I see you had
a question in the chat.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:43.080
Do you want to jump in here?

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:44.730
Yeah, sure.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:45.510
Hey, Emily.

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:48.630
By the way, great
presentation at [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:52.650
That was where I first got to
see you, so that was fantastic.

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:57.960
And my question was, what
do you do in your job

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:01.140
that you didn't actually
anticipate see yourself doing?

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:04.890
I think we all kind of get
blindsided by this weird niche

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:08.490
skill or task, and we're like, I
didn't sign up for this, right?

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:10.050
But I was just
curious because you've

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.270
been the Capital One
for a bit, do you

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:14.130
have anything like
that you could share?

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:16.190
Oh, my god.

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:18.540
If anything, I feel
like that's truly

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:21.680
the story of--
and to your point,

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:23.760
I don't think I'm
unique in this regard,

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:27.950
but I think does almost feel
like it summarizes my career

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:28.640
arc.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:31.310
Went from math to
statistics because I

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:34.010
wanted to do math
through the real world,

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.700
but then found the shift
from like deductive

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:40.280
to inductive reasoning
not at all like math.

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:42.740
Then went from stats
to data science

00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:49.190
because I wanted to use the
statistical toolbox to solve

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:52.380
these beautiful,
probabilistic-type problems,

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:54.560
but then found all
of my challenges

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:57.260
were more like data
and tooling layer.

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:00.928
And so, I mean, I think it's--

00:16:00.928 --> 00:16:03.130
this may be a kind
of generic answer,

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:08.110
but I think being obsessed
with not only data quality but,

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:11.440
even upstream of that, how
is data getting collected?

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:13.450
How do the source
systems work that are

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:15.400
processing the
customer records that

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:17.230
are then spitting out the data?

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:19.990
I think I definitely
never expected

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:25.550
myself to keep going further
upstream and further back

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:29.080
into more of the
technical stack as opposed

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:31.660
to proving a nice
little theorem, which

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:33.280
is where I started out.

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:39.920
But I think definitely
something where

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:41.900
I think a lot of
my mind share goes

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:45.860
but that I find is super
helpful for the last mile

00:16:45.860 --> 00:16:49.880
because even if some really
weird quirk in your system

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:53.540
of like, oh, we were
never able to email

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:56.750
these customers for x reasons,
it's, suddenly, then you

00:16:56.750 --> 00:17:00.770
also know population,
that something happened to

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:06.000
for a systemic reason that could
fuel some really interesting

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:09.119
analysis.

00:17:09.119 --> 00:17:11.720
So I think the both
going upstream but then

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:14.960
being able to roundtrip
back downstream

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:20.210
are probably two things that I
never would have anticipated.

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:21.170
Yeah, cool.

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:22.670
It's funny you share that.

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:25.400
I recently was told that
Data Science were snobs.

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:30.470
Like, we're only happy with the
tail end of the data stream.

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:32.450
I quickly had to
snap my head around

00:17:32.450 --> 00:17:34.550
and say, well, I think
we're, if anything, more

00:17:34.550 --> 00:17:38.240
holistically-- we're interested
in the holistic health of data

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.450
from start to finish, literally
nurturing it from its inception

00:17:41.450 --> 00:17:43.370
all the way to the end.

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:45.090
But I found that
really interesting.

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:46.970
So it's funny that
you're reiterating that,

00:17:46.970 --> 00:17:50.000
that you find yourself looking
downstream just as much

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.040
as you are upstream
and where you're at.

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:53.420
Anyway, thanks for sharing.

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:54.770
I'll shut up now.

00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:55.520
Not at all.

00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:58.040
And, yeah, I love that.

00:17:58.040 --> 00:17:59.510
I mean, to me, I
think that's also

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:02.570
part of what really puts
the science in data science,

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:05.390
is seeing the whole system.

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:08.420
I forget-- someone
in the "college-y"

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:11.870
side of our stats Twitter once
commented on something I wrote

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:16.010
about, oh, like, the
data-generating process--

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:18.620
I have all these same
concerns as you do.

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:21.080
It just, for me, it's
when I fell out of a boat,

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:22.280
and I dropped my notepad.

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:27.070
And it's very different
cause of missing data

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:32.090
but potentially similar outcome.

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.535
I love that, putting the
science in data science.

00:18:34.535 --> 00:18:38.530
[LAUGHS] Alan, I see you had
a question in the chat, too.

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:39.860
Want to jump in next?

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:40.880
Yeah, sure.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:41.900
Hi, Emily.

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:43.910
I'm really curious to
learn a little bit more

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:49.670
about where your team fits in,
if there's a bigger analytics

00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:51.320
organization in which you fit.

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:55.730
And do you have peers who are
similar leaders of analytics

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:58.200
within other
functions and teams?

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:01.370
And if you collaborate, and
what that expectation is,

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:05.245
or if your focus is mostly
kind of inward to your team?

00:19:05.245 --> 00:19:07.370
And I know it's probably
a mix of all of the above.

00:19:07.370 --> 00:19:10.700
I'm just really curious
in how your team fits

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:15.770
in the rest of an organization
that I imagine is really big.

00:19:15.770 --> 00:19:16.370
Yeah.

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:23.210
No, and I'll definitely again
dance around that potentially a

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:28.310
tiny bit, just to not talk
to too much about Capital One

00:19:28.310 --> 00:19:29.120
