00:20:57.960 --> 00:21:01.450
to the table when you have
different types of people

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:07.390
and different org configurations
all trying to tackle problems,

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:10.596
bring the best spare
of their own tool deck.

00:21:10.596 --> 00:21:11.705
For sure, yeah.

00:21:11.705 --> 00:21:13.080
And your journey
sounds like it's

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:17.760
been one of building that
really focused team in part.

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:19.090
Yeah, definitely.

00:21:19.090 --> 00:21:24.030
And I think, based on, I
think, the biggest bet,

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:26.250
that the people
that were maybe--

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:29.460
I mean, like me, starting out
a little bit more downstream,

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:32.670
maybe not the best
state-- like, not

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:37.410
the best engineer by a mile
that you could put on that work,

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:42.450
that finding people that can
bridge those multiple worlds

00:21:42.450 --> 00:21:49.330
and pull that downstream
context upstream, I think

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:57.280
is maybe not what I'd say
is the overarching norm,

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:59.440
but I think is
definitely a pattern that

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:04.880
is getting some good traction.

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:06.800
Cool, thank you.

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:08.430
EMILY RIEDERER: Thank you.

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:12.260
I see Javier is calling out
your amazing book collection

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:13.130
behind you.

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:17.030
And so Javier said it looks
like data science book heaven.

00:22:17.030 --> 00:22:19.670
Do you have a favorite
book behind you?

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:20.750
Oh, my goodness.

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:23.180
That would be a
hard one to pick,

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:28.250
and we could use the rest of
the hour going through those.

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:32.810
One of my weirdest or
strangest post-pandemic hobbies

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:35.360
has been I had the
opportunity to review

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:40.010
a ton of books for CRC Press,
and then, as a consequence,

00:22:40.010 --> 00:22:44.510
have built out my library
with a lot of those.

00:22:44.510 --> 00:22:48.290
I think, off the top
of my mind, obviously,

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:52.460
[INAUDIBLE] Rethinking
Statistics I know is a little

00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:59.970
bit of a generic recommendation,
but I think can just be such

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:00.720
a lovely--

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:03.150
whether or not
you're [INAUDIBLE]..

00:23:03.150 --> 00:23:05.430
Won't get controversial
here, but it's just

00:23:05.430 --> 00:23:09.750
such a beautiful
probabilistic approach

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:11.415
to thinking about
data and modeling.

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:17.700
I have a lot of
epidemiology books,

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:22.790
just because I but never was
really in the health sciences

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:25.820
field myself, but I
found those to actually

00:23:25.820 --> 00:23:30.380
be some of the most informative,
strangely, for business use

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:32.450
because I think stats
books often talk

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:34.580
about things from the
standpoint of like,

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:36.680
assume you have this
perfectly clean data.

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:38.630
It's in this matrix called x.

00:23:38.630 --> 00:23:41.060
Now we'll prove
some nice theorems.

00:23:41.060 --> 00:23:44.780
Whereas epi books almost
start more with like,

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:45.570
[INAUDIBLE] fire.

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:46.760
We want to figure something out.

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:48.343
We don't have any
of the data we want.

00:23:48.343 --> 00:23:49.640
There are biases everywhere.

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:51.210
What do we do given that.

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:55.610
And I think like,
again, when you're just

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:58.700
trying to work with a lot
of larger observational data

00:23:58.700 --> 00:24:02.810
in an org setting, that's
just super pragmatic.

00:24:02.810 --> 00:24:06.990
What can I do with what I have
available, is pretty cool.

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:14.760
But again, could definitely pass
along a lot more specific recs

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:17.177
based on the category.

00:24:17.177 --> 00:24:19.510
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: What was the
