Statistical Rethinking.

00:24:24.838 --> 00:24:26.380
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
OK, If anybody wants

00:24:26.380 --> 00:24:28.550
to share the link
to that in the chat,

00:24:28.550 --> 00:24:31.360
that would be
super helpful, too.

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:33.910
But Emily, something
I wanted to ask you

00:24:33.910 --> 00:24:37.960
is, I know so many people
here in our Hangout room

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:41.350
have benefited from your
blogs, and conference talks,

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:42.380
and so much more.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:45.860
And I was just
wondering, for you,

00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:48.190
what have been some of the
benefits of contributing

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:51.060
to the open source community?

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:53.860
EMILY RIEDERER: Oh my
goodness, like so very many.

00:24:53.860 --> 00:24:55.830
I think first and
foremost, obviously,

00:24:55.830 --> 00:25:00.090
it's just all of the amazing
relationships you form,

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:02.250
people you get to
meet, and being

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:05.930
a fly on the wall
of conversations

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:09.410
that I'd never get to
hear-- like epi professors

00:25:09.410 --> 00:25:12.710
talk, or bio stats professors
talk, or econ professors

00:25:12.710 --> 00:25:14.840
talk in my day-to-day life.

00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:18.260
But just being able
to continue to pull--

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:19.880
and I think this is
especially unique

00:25:19.880 --> 00:25:23.480
to ours from all these
really rich interdisciplinary

00:25:23.480 --> 00:25:26.750
communities and
steal the best ideas,

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:28.700
and then take them
back to my day job.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:32.120
And then I look like I came up
with a new idea, and I didn't.

00:25:32.120 --> 00:25:34.520
I'm just feeling like an artist.

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:39.620
So I think that the people
is definitely a huge benefit.

00:25:39.620 --> 00:25:43.100
In terms of contributions,
I think something

00:25:43.100 --> 00:25:48.790
that I found a very
surprising benefit is

00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:52.540
the way that it's made me
have to think a lot more

00:25:52.540 --> 00:25:55.930
critically and a lot more
formally about what I was doing

00:25:55.930 --> 00:25:57.550
anyway and otherwise.

00:25:57.550 --> 00:26:02.700
I think even as I've dodged
a couple of questions

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:06.570
here today of, oh,
how much detail can I

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:09.330
go into here, the
upside of that is,

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:14.050
I think, a lot of the things
that I've found that I can

00:26:14.050 --> 00:26:18.580
really lean in to talk
about, it forces me to think

00:26:18.580 --> 00:26:20.710
about the problems that
I'm facing at that higher

00:26:20.710 --> 00:26:23.962
level of abstraction
and think about what,

00:26:23.962 --> 00:26:25.420
have I learned from
this that's not

00:26:25.420 --> 00:26:29.810
just true to my last project,
but it's my next project,

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:32.490
and it's true to other
people's projects?

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:37.870
So I think it's definitely a
fun intellectual challenge,

00:26:37.870 --> 00:26:42.280
I think almost like taking
a final exam in school,

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:45.190
to force you to formalize
your own knowledge.

00:26:47.498 --> 00:26:49.540
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: That's
really interesting, yeah.

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:52.180
So by not being able to
give some of the specifics,

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:54.610
you're able to then take
a step back and look

00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:57.550
at this big picture,
so that it can then

00:26:57.550 --> 00:26:58.775
help other people, too.

00:26:58.775 --> 00:27:00.400
EMILY RIEDERER: Think
a little bit more

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:04.070
