I think it was back
in 2018 or 2017.

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:21.270
It's been a while.

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:24.270
But David Robinson
gave an amazing talk

00:28:24.270 --> 00:28:26.250
at our studio
conference about this

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:28.530
that's probably floating
around in a video

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:30.840
somewhere that I
know I personally

00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:32.590
found really empowering.

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:36.750
I think the first and
foremost advice, I think,

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:39.410
is get it out there.

00:28:39.410 --> 00:28:41.180
Anything that's out
in the world, I think,

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:43.430
was really the
thesis of his talk,

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:46.490
is better than anything
that's on your hard drive.

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:50.940
And I think that can at
times be intimidating.

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:56.840
But I think, a, I
think a lot of work

00:28:56.840 --> 00:28:59.840
can be a lot more
impressive and interesting

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:01.820
to the outside world
than sometimes we

00:29:01.820 --> 00:29:03.920
even believe when
we've been mile deep

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:06.120
working on it for so long.

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:09.290
And also, especially in a great
community like the ERstats

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:11.240
community, I feel
like people are

00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:14.370
really supportive that,
yeah, they might have

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:15.970
some advice or other ideas.

00:29:15.970 --> 00:29:17.910
So maybe they leave a comment.

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:21.300
But everyone does it in
such a genuinely nice way,

00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:24.630
if anything, sometimes
I crave for somebody

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:27.760
to comment on something
that I wrote and be like,

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:29.730
no, you're wrong, and
here's 10 reasons why,

00:29:29.730 --> 00:29:32.280
because I think that'd be a
really interesting discussion.

00:29:32.280 --> 00:29:35.370
But I think, A, definitely
don't be intimidated

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:37.450
to get stuff out there.

00:29:37.450 --> 00:29:46.700
And then, I think the
second key thing is, again,

00:29:46.700 --> 00:29:49.760
finding projects that you're
like genuinely interested in

00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:51.500
and passionate by.

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:54.860
I think a lot of people--

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:56.690
there's decreasing
marginal returns

00:29:56.690 --> 00:29:59.720
from an employer's
perspective seeing someone

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:02.930
else play with the
Titanic data set,

00:30:02.930 --> 00:30:06.870
whereas like I think
you'll showcase

00:30:06.870 --> 00:30:12.330
your own skills better, as well
as really engage your audience,

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:15.270
if you just chase
after some passion

00:30:15.270 --> 00:30:18.328
and find something a little
bit new and different to bring

00:30:18.328 --> 00:30:18.870
to the table.

00:30:23.400 --> 00:30:25.600
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Thank you.

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:30.880
I think I missed one of your
questions from earlier, Travis,

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:34.587
and I just want to
have you jump in here.

00:30:34.587 --> 00:30:35.295
Sorry about that.

00:30:35.295 --> 00:30:35.920
TRAVIS GERKE: Yeah, no worries.

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:37.680
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: I think that
maybe it was a while ago.

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:39.097
TRAVIS GERKE: Yeah,
no, it's fine.

00:30:39.097 --> 00:30:41.577
Hey, Emily, sorry to break
your level of abstraction

00:30:41.577 --> 00:30:42.660
that you've built up here.

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:45.647
But I wanted to ask
a bit about DPT.

00:30:45.647 --> 00:30:47.730
I know we talked about
this a couple of years ago.

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:50.113
TRAVIS GERKE: I wonder how
that's evolved for you.

00:30:50.113 --> 00:30:51.677
Are you using it
still these days?

00:30:51.677 --> 00:30:53.010
Have you integrated more with R?

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:54.635
You have the DPT
Flyer package, which I

00:30:54.635 --> 00:30:59.370
see is just hanging out there.

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:02.940
Trying to generate interest
across the community--

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:04.800
it's a really cool
complementary tool,

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:08.340
but not a lot of integrations
yet as far as I can tell.

00:31:08.340 --> 00:31:09.300
How's it going for you?

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:10.860
EMILY RIEDERER:
Yeah, no, I'm still--

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:14.400
and maybe I'll just take
a step back and explain

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:19.230
for a minute what dbt is for
those that aren't familiar.

00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:23.760
dbt is a framework for
building data pipelines in SQL.

00:31:23.760 --> 00:31:27.900
And what it gets you is a
lot of the same benefits

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:30.360
that I think we all value
with reproducibility

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:35.670
in R, which tends to be harder
with databases and with dbt.

00:31:35.670 --> 00:31:41.220
So you can do things
like devprod deployments.

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:42.960
You can do better testing.

00:31:42.960 --> 00:31:45.070
It helps you modulize
your code better

00:31:45.070 --> 00:31:50.340
so you can have easier version
control, easier code reviews.

00:31:50.340 --> 00:31:54.300
And it just ends with some
[INAUDIBLE] templating.

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:57.570
It makes SQL more like
a higher-level kind

00:31:57.570 --> 00:32:01.410
of programming language with
a legitimate control flow.

00:32:01.410 --> 00:32:03.690
Definitely, if you
work on databases,

00:32:03.690 --> 00:32:05.100
I'd recommend checking it out.

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:09.810
I think there's a lot of
spiritual similarity with just

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:13.980
things this community tends
to value and get excited by.

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:18.090
I continue to be a big
fan of using both tools.

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:20.070
I'd have to say, I've
never really found

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:24.960
that sweet spot of exactly
learning how to integrate them.

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:33.030
I think I still tend to have
dbt pipelines joining more

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:35.400
into the Database
Player, but kind

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:42.670
of switching more
into R mode for real,

00:32:42.670 --> 00:32:48.810
obviously, [INAUDIBLE] analysis,
reporting, modeling, et cetera.

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:51.900
I've always felt like there had
to be some linkage, especially

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:59.710
with something like dbflyer,
but have not gone there yet,

00:32:59.710 --> 00:33:01.630
and would be
fascinated if anyone

00:33:01.630 --> 00:33:06.672
else has played with both
and has any takes there.

00:33:06.672 --> 00:33:07.880
TRAVIS GERKE: Yeah, likewise.

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:09.140
In all your spare time, right?

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:12.650
[LAUGHS] And I'm sure everyone
else is in the same space.

00:33:12.650 --> 00:33:14.420
But yeah, my experience
has been the same.

00:33:14.420 --> 00:33:16.970
I feel like when I'm in dvtm,
it's straight SQL world,

00:33:16.970 --> 00:33:18.680
and there's no direct linkage.

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:21.650
But mirroring your thoughts
on the spiritual similarity.

00:33:21.650 --> 00:33:23.790
The community there is
really, really good,

00:33:23.790 --> 00:33:26.920
and it's a good tool if
you want to check it out.

00:33:26.920 --> 00:33:28.670
EMILY RIEDERER: Yeah,
and at minimum, it's

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:32.000
like it helps kind of keep
your mind in the same place,

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:38.720
even if there's not quite
that deep, deep integration.

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:39.880
TRAVIS GERKE: Yeah, thanks.

00:33:39.880 --> 00:33:41.255
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Sounds like this

00:33:41.255 --> 00:33:44.890
would be a good part to share
with the Tidyverse team, too.

00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:48.385
EMILY RIEDERER: Yeah,
let's [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:48.385 --> 00:33:51.302
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
[LAUGHS] So I see--

00:33:51.302 --> 00:33:52.760
so I just want to
remind everybody,

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:53.980
if you joined a
little bit later,

00:33:53.980 --> 00:33:55.790
that you can ask questions
a few different ways.

00:33:55.790 --> 00:33:57.250
So there's a Slido
link if you want

00:33:57.250 --> 00:33:58.990
to ask anything anonymously.

00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:02.350
You can always raise
your hand here on Zoom.

00:34:02.350 --> 00:34:04.180
Or, you can continue
putting questions

00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:06.700
into the Zoom chat too.

00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:08.500
And if you put a
little star next to it,

00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:10.840
I'll know to read it out loud.

00:34:10.840 --> 00:34:14.050
But I see [INAUDIBLE]
had asked, piggybacking

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:17.380
on Libby and Mylene's earlier
questions, what has kept you

00:34:17.380 --> 00:34:21.183
at Capital One for seven years?

00:34:21.183 --> 00:34:22.850
EMILY RIEDERER: That's
a great question.

00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:24.520
I know especially
in this day and age,

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:27.659
I think it's the
less common route

00:34:27.659 --> 00:34:31.139
to stay at a single
company for a long time.

00:34:31.139 --> 00:34:36.280
I think there's definite, I
think, pros and cons to it.

00:34:36.280 --> 00:34:39.370
I think definitely, the thing
that's kept me motivated

00:34:39.370 --> 00:34:43.989
is being able to continue
to expand my scope,

00:34:43.989 --> 00:34:47.500
expand my skill set, both in
terms of such a large company

00:34:47.500 --> 00:34:51.370
having different areas of
business to move around too--

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:56.050
which is, so I'm still thinking
about different strategic

00:34:56.050 --> 00:34:58.390
issues throughout
my career, somewhat

00:34:58.390 --> 00:35:04.630
similar to changing companies,
as well as growing out

00:35:04.630 --> 00:35:07.040
my technical skills in
different dimensions.

00:35:07.040 --> 00:35:10.090
So I think I'm
definitely somebody

00:35:10.090 --> 00:35:15.560
that gets very edgy if I
don't feel like I'm continuing

00:35:15.560 --> 00:35:17.540
to learn, but I
think I've found ways

00:35:17.540 --> 00:35:20.500
that I really feel
like I continue to be

00:35:20.500 --> 00:35:23.690
able to do that in my career.

00:35:23.690 --> 00:35:30.300
And secondly-- this is
a very silly reason,

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:32.370
definitely not a
career-defining one--

00:35:32.370 --> 00:35:35.460
but going back to [INAUDIBLE]
source, I will warn you,

00:35:35.460 --> 00:35:37.470
packages are kind
of like children,

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:39.300
and there have been
times in my career,

00:35:39.300 --> 00:35:41.490
even, where I've been
offered a different role

00:35:41.490 --> 00:35:44.970
internally or something,
and I've been like,

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:48.330
oh, but then I'm just like, I
don't know how to say goodbye.

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:51.984
So be warned.

00:35:51.984 --> 00:35:55.320
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
[LAUGHS] Thank you.

00:35:55.320 --> 00:35:58.830
Trevor, I see you had
a question in the chat.

00:35:58.830 --> 00:36:01.416
You want to jump in next?

00:36:01.416 --> 00:36:02.560
TREVOR: Hi, everyone.

00:36:02.560 --> 00:36:04.270
Good day.

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:09.010
Yeah, so I've been following
this space very carefully,

00:36:09.010 --> 00:36:12.820
and I've been a part of several
discussions about the impact

00:36:12.820 --> 00:36:20.250
that AI is and will
have on our profession.

00:36:20.250 --> 00:36:25.095
I must say I'm just uncertain
about the future, to be honest.

00:36:27.670 --> 00:36:32.020
It's developing and
evolving so fast,

00:36:32.020 --> 00:36:34.090
so I was just
trying to understand

00:36:34.090 --> 00:36:38.680
from your perspective, what
do you see how AI is actually

00:36:38.680 --> 00:36:43.630
impacting your current workflow,
and what your thoughts are

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:48.070
in terms of how it would impact
data science space in the mid

00:36:48.070 --> 00:36:48.610
to--

00:36:48.610 --> 00:36:52.662
well, near to mid future?

00:36:52.662 --> 00:36:55.120
EMILY RIEDERER: Yeah, no, that
is such an interesting issue

00:36:55.120 --> 00:36:55.690
right now.

00:36:55.690 --> 00:37:02.830
And I feel like definitely,
I know a lot of very strongly

00:37:02.830 --> 00:37:05.320
opinionated-- strong
opinions on both sides

00:37:05.320 --> 00:37:07.960
of the spectrum of whether
it's going to change everything

00:37:07.960 --> 00:37:09.160
or nothing.

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:14.020
I think through the
type of work I do,

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:21.030
and the type of values, per se,
that I have about data science,

00:37:21.030 --> 00:37:23.070
I think there are
large parts of it

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:25.500
that I don't expect
it to change a ton,

00:37:25.500 --> 00:37:30.150
or I almost hope it
doesn't change a ton.

00:37:30.150 --> 00:37:32.490
And I'll give a couple
of examples of that.

00:37:32.490 --> 00:37:34.590
I know I think,
even, there are a lot

00:37:34.590 --> 00:37:36.900
of companies and
tools right now that

00:37:36.900 --> 00:37:41.280
are working on automated
NLP query generation--

00:37:41.280 --> 00:37:44.880
which, on one hand,
seems really cool.

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:47.850
Yeah, I'd love to be able to
just answer anyone question,

00:37:47.850 --> 00:37:50.980
ask a question
and get an answer.

00:37:50.980 --> 00:37:54.330
But then, there's, I think, the
data quality part in my head.

00:37:54.330 --> 00:37:57.760
It's like the fire
alarm goes off.

00:37:57.760 --> 00:38:08.030
That, there may be an AI that's
like smart enough to write

00:38:08.030 --> 00:38:11.330
a sonnet far sooner than there
is one that's smart enough

00:38:11.330 --> 00:38:15.740
to think, based on the kind of
conditional probability this

00:38:15.740 --> 00:38:19.310
person allotted, should I cast
the nulls in this table to a 0,

00:38:19.310 --> 00:38:21.650
or should I leave them as 0's?

00:38:21.650 --> 00:38:23.870
And what should be
in the denominator?

00:38:23.870 --> 00:38:28.220
And I mean, I think, if
anything, data quality and data

00:38:28.220 --> 00:38:32.570
bias will save us, because I
think those are so sufficiently

00:38:32.570 --> 00:38:36.290
hard for humans to
ever be precise enough

00:38:36.290 --> 00:38:38.450
in language about
what they mean,

00:38:38.450 --> 00:38:49.780
and clear enough on what
the right result should be,

00:38:49.780 --> 00:38:53.630
that I feel pretty
confident that's

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:58.940
going to remain a solidly human
problem for a much longer time.

00:38:58.940 --> 00:39:02.030
And then, similarly, on the last
mile, like I mentioned before,

00:39:02.030 --> 00:39:07.490
I think I'm very passionate
about thinking about feature

00:39:07.490 --> 00:39:11.270
engineering, curating
both automated solutions,

00:39:11.270 --> 00:39:15.470
but also understanding,
and bringing context

00:39:15.470 --> 00:39:16.890
in to data science--

00:39:16.890 --> 00:39:21.770
whether you even think about
cost-sensitive loss functions,

00:39:21.770 --> 00:39:25.070
model evaluation beyond
traditional metrics.

00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:28.010
And I could imagine
more guesswork shifting

00:39:28.010 --> 00:39:34.020
to those first mile and
last mile of data quality,

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:40.750
and duration, and rigorous
business-focused evaluation,

00:39:40.750 --> 00:39:42.940
but I definitely
don't expect our jobs

00:39:42.940 --> 00:39:47.596
to go away because of AI.

00:39:47.596 --> 00:39:49.480
TREVOR: I understand
what you're saying,

00:39:49.480 --> 00:39:53.890
but even a lot of the
processes that you talk about,

00:39:53.890 --> 00:39:58.410
we see AI making its
way into that space.

00:39:58.410 --> 00:40:02.630
I understand, and I do
totally agree with your point

00:40:02.630 --> 00:40:05.300
about, for example,
the feeding of data.

00:40:05.300 --> 00:40:08.180
An AI system may
not know the context

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:11.430
of what a missing value should
be or why a piece of data

00:40:11.430 --> 00:40:11.930
is missing.

00:40:11.930 --> 00:40:15.080
But my thought process is a
little broader in the sense

00:40:15.080 --> 00:40:19.610
that, for example, as
someone asked earlier,

00:40:19.610 --> 00:40:22.008
what you should be
thinking about when

00:40:22.008 --> 00:40:23.300
you're entering the data space?

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:27.050
As in, if you're in school now,
you are in, maybe, university,

00:40:27.050 --> 00:40:31.610
you're in the middle of
doing a data science degree,

00:40:31.610 --> 00:40:37.250
what does your future look like?

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:41.020
So we have seen, for example, in
the last, maybe, 5 to 10 years,

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:44.980
last five years,
we have seen where

00:40:44.980 --> 00:40:48.070
data science is the hottest
topic and the hottest job.

00:40:50.950 --> 00:40:55.240
Do we think that this
hot streak is going

00:40:55.240 --> 00:40:56.410
to continue into the future?

00:40:56.410 --> 00:41:00.610
Or will this hot streak
be replaced by AI,

00:41:00.610 --> 00:41:03.550
if you understand
what I'm asking?

00:41:03.550 --> 00:41:04.970
EMILY RIEDERER:
Yeah, definitely.

00:41:04.970 --> 00:41:06.610
I mean, at the end
of the day, I think

00:41:06.610 --> 00:41:11.000
they're probably all
just tools in the toolbox

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:14.660
to solve the super
wide range of problems.

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:18.090
I don't even know if
data science will always

00:41:18.090 --> 00:41:21.480
be a career, so
much as data science

00:41:21.480 --> 00:41:28.020
is an expectation of
something professionals

00:41:28.020 --> 00:41:30.600
should be able to apply to the
problems they have at hand,

00:41:30.600 --> 00:41:34.050
like manipulating an
Excel spreadsheet.

00:41:34.050 --> 00:41:36.780
And similarly, with AI, I
think there are probably

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:41.460
some things, like
in a legal setting,

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:45.660
like parsing a ton of documents,
generating some hypotheses,

00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:49.410
helping maybe structure
a preliminary screen

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:53.160
for legal discovery
or something.

00:41:53.160 --> 00:41:55.830
I think there's some
kind of assistive things

00:41:55.830 --> 00:42:00.300
AI can help with,
but I personally,

00:42:00.300 --> 00:42:03.240
I think, don't love
the idea of a world

00:42:03.240 --> 00:42:05.740
where it's ever making
the final decisions.

00:42:05.740 --> 00:42:11.130
So I think plenty of space
at the table for both.

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:12.550
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Yeah, definitely.

00:42:12.550 --> 00:42:13.350
TREVOR: OK.

00:42:13.350 --> 00:42:15.240
All right, thanks.

00:42:15.240 --> 00:42:16.410
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Thank you.

00:42:16.410 --> 00:42:20.550
I love that perspective
on it, and thinking

00:42:20.550 --> 00:42:23.580
about how everybody knows
how to use Excel right now.

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:25.800
Maybe at some point
in the future,

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:31.020
everybody will know how to do
some level of data science.

00:42:31.020 --> 00:42:35.105
I see, Jonathan, you had
asked a question on--

00:42:35.105 --> 00:42:36.480
there's a star,
so I'll read it--

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:39.630
but it's, data science involves
tackling complex problems.

00:42:39.630 --> 00:42:42.660
What is your thought
process to solving a problem

00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:45.720
that you don't know how
to solve immediately?

00:42:45.720 --> 00:42:48.240
What resources do you
into that assist you

00:42:48.240 --> 00:42:49.500
with problem-solving?

00:42:52.390 --> 00:42:54.430
EMILY RIEDERER: That's
a really good question

00:42:54.430 --> 00:42:56.950
and a really, like,
pertinent question, lately, I

00:42:56.950 --> 00:42:59.540
think, for a couple
of different reasons.

00:42:59.540 --> 00:43:06.720
I think one thing that I think
is a really undervalued part

00:43:06.720 --> 00:43:12.150
of that process is
thinking about what is--

00:43:12.150 --> 00:43:14.650
how will a good solution
when you find fun one,

00:43:14.650 --> 00:43:16.650
and how would you know
if it was a good solution

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:19.770
if it was staring you in the
face and you already had it?

00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:23.440
I think the more
unstructured and complicated

00:43:23.440 --> 00:43:28.260
a problem can be, it can almost
be a little deceptive of what's

00:43:28.260 --> 00:43:35.957
good-- which can have one or two
bad outcomes of either you find

00:43:35.957 --> 00:43:37.540
a good solution, but
you don't realize

00:43:37.540 --> 00:43:39.790
it's good so you
keep going, or you

00:43:39.790 --> 00:43:43.340
spend a lot of time
chasing after an outcome,

00:43:43.340 --> 00:43:47.710
and then you get there, and
only then do you realize,

00:43:47.710 --> 00:43:49.480
I solved the problem
I was trying to stop,

00:43:49.480 --> 00:43:51.920
but not the problem
I wanted to solve.

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:55.000
So something I've really
been experimenting

00:43:55.000 --> 00:43:59.770
with in my own work is having a
lot more of an explicit design

00:43:59.770 --> 00:44:03.520
stage at the beginning of
a project and thinking,

00:44:03.520 --> 00:44:04.930
how can you do a pilot?

00:44:04.930 --> 00:44:10.370
How can you-- if I'm trying
to predict some target,

00:44:10.370 --> 00:44:13.700
can I take those two
values of that target

00:44:13.700 --> 00:44:16.640
and plug them into a
downstream problem I actually

00:44:16.640 --> 00:44:18.110
thought that I was
going to solve,

00:44:18.110 --> 00:44:20.810
and make sure that's actually
what I want to solve?

00:44:20.810 --> 00:44:25.640
And almost like frontloading
model evaluation

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:28.880
with even fake solution,
is like the first step

00:44:28.880 --> 00:44:29.915
versus last step.

00:44:32.480 --> 00:44:35.340
And then, I'll check
on one other point.

00:44:35.340 --> 00:44:38.330
And I'm rambling here, but
I think the other aspect

00:44:38.330 --> 00:44:40.700
of that-- just, again,
going back to that levels

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:42.230
of abstraction--

00:44:42.230 --> 00:44:45.140
is figuring out how to
take the context out

00:44:45.140 --> 00:44:49.490
of my problem to make it
something more Googleable.

00:44:49.490 --> 00:44:54.830
So I mean thinking,
not being like,

00:44:54.830 --> 00:44:57.520
oh, this experiment, the
random seeds were wrong,

00:44:57.520 --> 00:44:59.710
so I don't have a
control population.

00:44:59.710 --> 00:45:00.550
What do I do?

00:45:00.550 --> 00:45:02.890
But then, backing that
into more of a general,

00:45:02.890 --> 00:45:06.430
like, how do you sample
a synthetic control

00:45:06.430 --> 00:45:07.650
through observational data?

00:45:07.650 --> 00:45:09.400
Which is something you
can Google and then

00:45:09.400 --> 00:45:11.050
find a ton of resources about.

00:45:11.050 --> 00:45:16.070
So I think pushing
myself on what I want,

00:45:16.070 --> 00:45:20.080
and then finding the
right [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:20.080 --> 00:45:21.630
at which to ask for help.

00:45:24.190 --> 00:45:27.030
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: That's
great, thank you.

00:45:27.030 --> 00:45:28.510
Ahnas, I see you had a question.

00:45:28.510 --> 00:45:29.885
Do you want to
jump in here next?

00:45:29.885 --> 00:45:31.290
AHNAS ALZAHABI: Sure.

00:45:31.290 --> 00:45:33.750
So Emily, I'm sure you've
been in this position before,

00:45:33.750 --> 00:45:36.450
but any tips and
advice on communicating

00:45:36.450 --> 00:45:41.040
to non-technical stakeholders
and/or leadership?

00:45:41.040 --> 00:45:44.340
EMILY RIEDERER: Yeah, no,
that's always the hot--

00:45:44.340 --> 00:45:47.590
enduring hot topic, and for
very, very good reasons,

00:45:47.590 --> 00:45:48.660
I think.

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:51.420
I think a couple of
different, I think,

00:45:51.420 --> 00:45:55.150
approaches that I tend
to use there is, A,

00:45:55.150 --> 00:45:59.897
reading with the
solution versus--

00:45:59.897 --> 00:46:01.480
leading with the
solution, and leading

00:46:01.480 --> 00:46:05.480
with impact, versus leading
with that kind of process.

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:07.630
And I think, as
many people know,

00:46:07.630 --> 00:46:09.910
I'm like huge Markdown fan--

00:46:09.910 --> 00:46:12.430
and maybe now I
should say Quarto--

00:46:12.430 --> 00:46:16.120
but I've actually come
to the realization

00:46:16.120 --> 00:46:18.760
that I need to even change
my workflow for working

00:46:18.760 --> 00:46:23.290
with a tool like that because
inherently, an Markdown

00:46:23.290 --> 00:46:25.450
workflow is very linear--

00:46:25.450 --> 00:46:28.360
like I cleaned my data,
I did the analysis,

00:46:28.360 --> 00:46:29.680
I got the outcome.

00:46:29.680 --> 00:46:32.850
But then, from a
storytelling perspective,

00:46:32.850 --> 00:46:34.800
I think you can
really get the hook

00:46:34.800 --> 00:46:39.030
and get the buy-in to keep that
conversation going if you lead

00:46:39.030 --> 00:46:42.680
more with the outcome of, this
is why what I'm about to tell

00:46:42.680 --> 00:46:45.210
you is important.

00:46:45.210 --> 00:46:48.170
And then, some people
want to really dig deeper

00:46:48.170 --> 00:46:50.820
and understand the mechanics.

00:46:50.820 --> 00:46:52.060
Some people won't.

00:46:52.060 --> 00:46:56.050
And I think it's also,
as a data person,

00:46:56.050 --> 00:47:00.250
they need to accept that that's
OK, but they don't want to.

00:47:00.250 --> 00:47:02.770
I think all of us,
it's like it can really

00:47:02.770 --> 00:47:05.020
be about the journey
versus the outcome,

00:47:05.020 --> 00:47:08.690
but accepting for like
leaders, reasonably,

00:47:08.690 --> 00:47:12.310
it is often about the outcome.

00:47:12.310 --> 00:47:15.040
But then, when I want to
go into those details,

00:47:15.040 --> 00:47:17.920
I think I both either
try to lean heavily

00:47:17.920 --> 00:47:25.380
on both and/or either of
metaphors and also diagrams,

00:47:25.380 --> 00:47:27.300
or one really compelling plot--

00:47:27.300 --> 00:47:31.620
just anything to
make it more tangible

00:47:31.620 --> 00:47:33.300
versus purely conceptual.

00:47:36.410 --> 00:47:37.558
AHNAS ALZAHABI: Thank you.

00:47:37.558 --> 00:47:39.100
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Thank you very much.

00:47:39.100 --> 00:47:43.040
I see, Louis, you had a
similar question as well.

00:47:43.040 --> 00:47:45.360
And do you have any
follow-up questions,

00:47:45.360 --> 00:47:49.418
or does that answer you as well?

00:47:49.418 --> 00:47:51.210
LOUIS BOGUCHWAL: I
guess maybe a follow-up,

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:52.890
and maybe it really is the same.

00:47:52.890 --> 00:47:54.990
Just to clarify, just
that, in one sense,

00:47:54.990 --> 00:47:59.730
makes it sound a
communication kind of piece.

00:47:59.730 --> 00:48:02.260
But do you have the
same answer if it's

00:48:02.260 --> 00:48:06.360
a brand new project, or maybe a
completely different approach?

00:48:06.360 --> 00:48:08.370
I don't mean a new
quantitative approach,

00:48:08.370 --> 00:48:10.830
but maybe, here's
a stakeholder who's

00:48:10.830 --> 00:48:13.140
not really worked with
quantitative people,

00:48:13.140 --> 00:48:15.900
or maybe is wedded to a toolset.

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:18.090
Maybe they use a business
intelligence tool,

00:48:18.090 --> 00:48:21.692
and you're advocating
Shiny, and it's interactive,

00:48:21.692 --> 00:48:22.650
or something like that.

00:48:22.650 --> 00:48:25.590
So to them, there's
some kind of context

00:48:25.590 --> 00:48:29.250
which, even if, let's say,
quantitatively there isn't, do

00:48:29.250 --> 00:48:33.070
you have any advice on how
to advocate that and get

00:48:33.070 --> 00:48:33.570
the buy-in?

00:48:33.570 --> 00:48:36.875
And, of course, I imagine it's
probably still a question of,

00:48:36.875 --> 00:48:38.250
here's why what
I'm about to tell

00:48:38.250 --> 00:48:40.260
you should be
interesting or helpful.

00:48:40.260 --> 00:48:41.880
But do you have
anything else that's

00:48:41.880 --> 00:48:43.797
come up where you say,
this has really worked,

00:48:43.797 --> 00:48:46.290
when I'm trying to
advocate something new?

00:48:46.290 --> 00:48:47.790
EMILY RIEDERER:
Yeah, no, I think

00:48:47.790 --> 00:48:53.010
that's a great point of
tools versus analyzes,

00:48:53.010 --> 00:48:57.070
because tools are so
inherently abstract,

00:48:57.070 --> 00:48:59.260
it can make it harder.

00:48:59.260 --> 00:49:04.940
But I do think probably
how I'd translate it,

00:49:04.940 --> 00:49:06.800
leaders inherently,
I think, care

00:49:06.800 --> 00:49:09.740
a lot about the
people on their team--

00:49:09.740 --> 00:49:12.780
hopefully from the genuinely
caring perspective,

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:15.500
but at least from like
managing capacity perspective,

00:49:15.500 --> 00:49:17.040
like bare minimum.

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:22.820
And I think like leading with
the case, I think, of, I think,

00:49:22.820 --> 00:49:25.610
most tools you can also
bring a human dimension

00:49:25.610 --> 00:49:27.470
into that story.

00:49:27.470 --> 00:49:32.010
Whether it's like-- it's such
a pain point that people have

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:35.020
to-- they're doing this manual
work every single month,

00:49:35.020 --> 00:49:38.892
then that's inhibiting both them
doing more interesting projects

00:49:38.892 --> 00:49:40.600
and their career
development, but there's

00:49:40.600 --> 00:49:42.210
a way to automate this thing.

00:49:42.210 --> 00:49:48.810
Or like, hey, three months
ago, we had this error and this

00:49:48.810 --> 00:49:52.860
breakdown of this process, and
that's because there aren't--

00:49:52.860 --> 00:49:56.100
we don't have good
version control on GitHub.

00:49:56.100 --> 00:50:00.510
And I think always,
you can tie it either

00:50:00.510 --> 00:50:03.550
to business outcomes
or people outcomes.

00:50:03.550 --> 00:50:08.860
But I always, I think, try
to anchor more to, what does

00:50:08.860 --> 00:50:12.170
any tool do for you?

00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:15.470
And there's a broader
framework that I really

00:50:15.470 --> 00:50:17.090
like for thinking
about this called

00:50:17.090 --> 00:50:21.500
The Jobs to Be Done framework
that comes from the product

00:50:21.500 --> 00:50:23.280
management world.

00:50:23.280 --> 00:50:25.610
And essentially,
what that says is

00:50:25.610 --> 00:50:28.070
you can think about
a tool as someone

00:50:28.070 --> 00:50:30.170
you're hiring to do a job.

00:50:30.170 --> 00:50:33.300
And I'm saying it at a
trivially high level.

00:50:33.300 --> 00:50:35.420
I think there's more
to the actual theory.

00:50:35.420 --> 00:50:42.157
But I've always liked
that kind of framework

00:50:42.157 --> 00:50:44.240
for thinking about the
interview-- thinking about,

00:50:44.240 --> 00:50:47.720
why should I like bother
to hire and onboard you?

00:50:47.720 --> 00:50:49.910
And is this tool more like--

00:50:49.910 --> 00:50:52.220
almost going back to
the AI discussion--

00:50:52.220 --> 00:50:53.990
is this like an
intern that I only

00:50:53.990 --> 00:50:56.750
want to use with a very
high level of supervision,

00:50:56.750 --> 00:51:00.290
or, if you're thinking about
CI/CD and process automation,

00:51:00.290 --> 00:51:04.400
it's like, am I hiring an
executive or a contractor

00:51:04.400 --> 00:51:09.000
to run this process for
me and abstract it way?

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:11.570
And I think that's like--
that framework, I think,

00:51:11.570 --> 00:51:16.780
is really, whether it's
implicit or explicit,

00:51:16.780 --> 00:51:20.455
hooked to my storytelling around
those technical tools also.

00:51:22.567 --> 00:51:24.150
LOUIS BOGUCHWAL:
Very cool, thank you.

00:51:24.150 --> 00:51:26.780
Thank you, very helpful.

00:51:26.780 --> 00:51:28.180
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Thank you.

00:51:28.180 --> 00:51:31.900
So I see we have a
few minutes left here,

00:51:31.900 --> 00:51:34.648
and I think still quite
a few questions here.

00:51:34.648 --> 00:51:36.940
So I'm going to go and try
and go through some of these

00:51:36.940 --> 00:51:37.960
quickly.

00:51:37.960 --> 00:51:40.840
But I see Bill had asked--

00:51:40.840 --> 00:51:42.970
he said, I'm in
pharma but wonder

00:51:42.970 --> 00:51:47.500
if there are trends blessed
by management to move away

00:51:47.500 --> 00:51:53.200
from some alternative software--
which is how Bill says SaaS.

00:51:53.200 --> 00:51:57.670
But maybe more
broadly, are there

00:51:57.670 --> 00:52:00.460
trends from your leadership
like moving towards open source?

00:52:00.460 --> 00:52:04.470
Or do you have free reign over
the tools that you get to use?

00:52:04.470 --> 00:52:06.340
EMILY RIEDERER:
Yeah, so I definitely

00:52:06.340 --> 00:52:09.250
feel really lucky to be
in an environment that's

00:52:09.250 --> 00:52:13.550
pretty much always been very,
very friendly to open source.

00:52:13.550 --> 00:52:15.190
And I think that is--

00:52:15.190 --> 00:52:17.410
I know a trend I've seen
across the industry.

00:52:17.410 --> 00:52:20.650
I've had the opportunity
also to do some consulting

00:52:20.650 --> 00:52:22.870
projects with other people--

00:52:22.870 --> 00:52:27.820
more into biostats and pharma
fields, taking that leap to--

00:52:32.120 --> 00:52:35.780
I'd copy Bill's very diplomatic
phrasing, if I could,

00:52:35.780 --> 00:52:42.170
but other proprietary
options to the R world.

00:52:42.170 --> 00:52:45.700
And especially, I mean, I
can only imagine, I think,

00:52:45.700 --> 00:52:50.020
the intersection of data tooling
with the current economic

00:52:50.020 --> 00:52:54.640
conditions, I have to imagine,
can only accelerate that trend

00:52:54.640 --> 00:52:58.510
when it's becoming an
increasingly easy-to-hire skill

00:52:58.510 --> 00:53:03.100
set, much more attractive
option for the bottom line.

00:53:03.100 --> 00:53:08.760
And I think that
gains that have been

00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:11.610
made in adoption in both
banking and finance,

00:53:11.610 --> 00:53:15.720
as well as health care,
really put a lot more

00:53:15.720 --> 00:53:18.840
of a rubber stamp of
approval on some version

00:53:18.840 --> 00:53:22.020
of open source in a really
highly regulated industry.

00:53:24.985 --> 00:53:26.110
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Thank you.

00:53:26.110 --> 00:53:29.500
And Amrit, I see you had a
question about transitioning

00:53:29.500 --> 00:53:30.700
into data science roles.

00:53:30.700 --> 00:53:32.532
Do you want to jump in here?

00:53:32.532 --> 00:53:34.260
AMRIT BHAT: Yeah, thank you.

00:53:34.260 --> 00:53:36.090
And hi, Emily.

00:53:36.090 --> 00:53:37.410
EMILY RIEDERER: Hey.

00:53:37.410 --> 00:53:40.140
AMRIT BHAT: Yeah,
so my basic question

00:53:40.140 --> 00:53:43.890
was that I am just transitioning
into data science roles,

00:53:43.890 --> 00:53:45.730
or I'm looking for
data science roles.

00:53:45.730 --> 00:53:49.110
So I was just wondering,
are there any considerations

00:53:49.110 --> 00:53:53.820
I should be having
while interviewing

00:53:53.820 --> 00:54:01.710
with companies, and in terms
of maximizing my capacity

00:54:01.710 --> 00:54:04.470
to learn in that company?

00:54:04.470 --> 00:54:07.320
Because I'll just give
you some more context.

00:54:07.320 --> 00:54:10.980
I was just talking to one of
my friends who is in the health

00:54:10.980 --> 00:54:13.980
care space, and she was
saying-- she's working as a data

00:54:13.980 --> 00:54:16.980
scientist, but then she was
saying that a lot of her time

00:54:16.980 --> 00:54:23.790
goes into data processing
rather than actually having to--

00:54:23.790 --> 00:54:26.820
being able to get any
inferences out of data

00:54:26.820 --> 00:54:30.880
or being able to do any
kind of predictive work.

00:54:30.880 --> 00:54:34.780
So yeah, I was just
wondering if there

00:54:34.780 --> 00:54:37.195
are any considerations
I should be having,

00:54:37.195 --> 00:54:39.580
or if this is a non-question.

00:54:39.580 --> 00:54:41.055
I'm really not sure.

00:54:41.055 --> 00:54:42.430
EMILY RIEDERER:
Yeah, no, I mean,

00:54:42.430 --> 00:54:46.610
I think there are three
aspects, I'd say, to that.

00:54:46.610 --> 00:54:51.680
I think first, it definitely can
be really helpful to understand

00:54:51.680 --> 00:54:54.470
a company's tech stack
when you're interviewing,

00:54:54.470 --> 00:54:56.720
and the amount of data
they have available--

00:54:56.720 --> 00:55:00.170
that you don't want to be
hired to be a data scientist,

00:55:00.170 --> 00:55:04.400
and then show up on day one and
have them be like, oh, yeah,

00:55:04.400 --> 00:55:06.710
here's a Google Drive
with some CSVs in it.

00:55:06.710 --> 00:55:08.250
Like, go wild.

00:55:08.250 --> 00:55:10.700
I mean, you want to be
sure they have enough data

00:55:10.700 --> 00:55:13.460
to support analysis,
and that either you'll

00:55:13.460 --> 00:55:16.190
be empowered to have the tools
to build out the data you need

00:55:16.190 --> 00:55:20.150
yourself, or maybe they
also have data engineering

00:55:20.150 --> 00:55:22.560
or other job functions.

00:55:22.560 --> 00:55:26.130
Secondly, I think it's helpful
to be crisp about what you want

00:55:26.130 --> 00:55:28.590
to learn and grow on in a role.

00:55:28.590 --> 00:55:32.490
Data science can be such a
nebulous job title these days.

00:55:32.490 --> 00:55:36.510
I mean, I think some places,
it can mean anything from DI,

00:55:36.510 --> 00:55:39.420
experimentation, modeling,
machine learning.

00:55:39.420 --> 00:55:43.830
And I think really, just
clarifying your interests,

00:55:43.830 --> 00:55:47.670
and then just being able
to articulate them--

00:55:47.670 --> 00:55:51.330
companies like do not have
any incentive to hire you

00:55:51.330 --> 00:55:52.530
for a role you don't want.

00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:57.550
So I think being able to share
what you're looking for really

00:55:57.550 --> 00:56:00.040
just helps matchmaking.

00:56:00.040 --> 00:56:03.100
But finally, I think it's
also really good to recognize,

00:56:03.100 --> 00:56:06.700
you can learn a ton in
pretty much any role.

00:56:06.700 --> 00:56:15.020
And I think the spending a lot
of time on data processing,

00:56:15.020 --> 00:56:18.500
taking that just as an
example, that is sometimes

00:56:18.500 --> 00:56:22.250
the hard part, the complex
part, the part that requires,

00:56:22.250 --> 00:56:24.500
actually, a lot of the
data science skill set

00:56:24.500 --> 00:56:29.560
of understanding, how do I
shape this data in a way that,

00:56:29.560 --> 00:56:33.490
because I do understand the
stats, because I do understand

00:56:33.490 --> 00:56:37.620
the algorithm, how do I
structure this problem in a way

00:56:37.620 --> 00:56:40.450
that the algorithm can
understand the problem?

00:56:40.450 --> 00:56:43.110
And at the end of
the day, I think

00:56:43.110 --> 00:56:46.450
it's a funny conceit
that in school, we

00:56:46.450 --> 00:56:48.700
spend most of our time
learning hyperparameter

00:56:48.700 --> 00:56:53.740
tuning and typing that
model.fit, model.predict.

00:56:53.740 --> 00:56:56.230
That often isn't the
